     nnaannoobbssdd..sshh — utility used to create a FreeBSD system image suitable for
     embedded applications

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnaannoobbssdd..sshh [--BBbbffhhIIiiKKkknnqqvvWWwwXX] [--cc _c_o_n_f_i_g_-_f_i_l_e]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnaannoobbssdd..sshh utility is a script which produces a minimal implementa‐
     tion of FreeBSD (called NNaannooBBSSDD), which typically fits on a small media
     such as a Compact Flash card, or other mass storage medium.  It can be
     used to build specialized install images, designed for easy installation
     and maintenance.

     The following options are available:

           --BB              Skip the install stages (both for kernel and
                           world).

           --bb              Skip the build stages (both for kernel and world).

           --cc _c_o_n_f_i_g_-_f_i_l_e  Specify the configuration file to use.

           --ff              Skip the code slice extraction.

           --hh              Display usage information.

           --II              Build the disk image from an existing build/in‐
                           stall.

           --ii              Skip the disk image build stage.

           --KK              Skip the iinnssttaallllkkeerrnneell stage of the build.

           --kk              Skip the bbuuiillddkkeerrnneell stage of the build.

           --nn              Do not cleanup before each build stage.  This sup‐
                           presses the normal cleanup work done before the
                           bbuuiillddwwoorrlldd stage and adds -DNO_CLEAN to the make
                           command line used for each build stage (world and
                           kernel).

           --qq              Make output more quiet.

           --vv              Make output more verbose.

           --WW              Skip the iinnssttaallllwwoorrlldd stage of the build.

           --ww              Skip the bbuuiillddwwoorrlldd stage of the build.

           --XX              Make nnaattiivvee--xxttoooollss.

     The features of NNaannooBBSSDD include:

           ••   Ports and packages work as in FreeBSD.  Every single applica‐
               tion can be installed and used in a NNaannooBBSSDD image, the same way
               as in FreeBSD.
           ••   No missing functionality.  If it is possible to do something
               with FreeBSD, it is possible to do the same thing with NNaannooBBSSDD,
               unless the specific feature or features were explicitly removed
               from the NNaannooBBSSDD image when it was created.
           ••   Everything is read-only at run-time.  It is safe to pull the
               power-plug.  There is no necessity to run fsck(8) after a non-
               graceful shutdown of the system.
           ••   Easy to build and customize.  Making use of just one shell
               script and one configuration file it is possible to build re‐
               duced and customized images satisfying any arbitrary set of re‐
               quirements.

   NNaannooBBSSDD MMeeddiiaa LLaayyoouutt
     The mass storage medium is divided into three parts by default (which are
     normally mounted read-only):

           ••   Two image partitions: code#1 and code#2.
           ••   The configuration file partition, which can be mounted under
               the _/_c_f_g directory at run time.

     The _/_e_t_c and _/_v_a_r directories are md(4) (malloc backed) disks.

     The configuration file partition persists under the _/_c_f_g directory.  It
     contains files for _/_e_t_c directory and is briefly mounted read-only right
     after the system boot, therefore it is required to copy modified files
     from _/_e_t_c back to the _/_c_f_g directory if changes are expected to persist
     after the system restarts.

BBUUIILLDDIINNGG NNaannooBBSSDD
     A NNaannooBBSSDD image is built using a simple nnaannoobbssdd..sshh shell script, which
     can be found in the _s_r_c_/_t_o_o_l_s_/_t_o_o_l_s_/_n_a_n_o_b_s_d directory.  This script cre‐
     ates a bootable image, which can be copied on the storage medium using
     the dd(1) utility.

     The necessary commands to build and install a NNaannooBBSSDD image are:

           cd /usr/src/tools/tools/nanobsd
           sh nanobsd.sh
           cd /usr/obj/nanobsd.full
           dd if=_.disk.full of=/dev/da0 bs=64k

CCUUSSTTOOMMIIZZIINNGG NNaannooBBSSDD
     This is probably the most important and most interesting feature of
     NNaannooBBSSDD.  This is also where you will be spending most of the time when
     developing with NNaannooBBSSDD.

     Customization is done in two ways:

           ••   Configuration options.
           ••   Custom functions.

     With configuration settings, it is possible to configure options passed
     to both the bbuuiillddwwoorrlldd and iinnssttaallllwwoorrlldd stages of the NNaannooBBSSDD build
     process, as well as internal options passed to the main build process of
     NNaannooBBSSDD.  Through these options it is possible to cut the system down, so
     it will fit on as little as 64MB.  You can use the configuration options
     to trim down the system even more, until it will consist of just the ker‐
     nel and two or three files in the userland.

     The configuration file consists of configuration options, which override
     the default values.  The most important directives are:

